        "coverage": "complete-chapter",
        "focusChunkId": null
      },
      "answer": "Chapter 1 introduces Mr. Jones, the negligent owner of Manor Farm, who neglects to secure the hens' pop-holes before going to bed drunk. As the humans sleep, the animals gather in the big barn to hear a speech from Old Major, a respected prize boar. Old Major shares his wisdom about the miserable and laborious lives animals endure under human tyranny, emphasizing that man is the true enemy who exploits them. He calls for rebellion to overthrow humans, advocating for animal unity and equality. He warns the animals not to adopt human vices after their victory. Major also sings \"Beasts of England,\" an inspiring revolutionary song about a future where animals live free and prosper. The animals enthusiastically sing along, but their noise awakens Mr. Jones, who fires a warning shot, ending the meeting. Thus, the chapter sets up the themes of oppression, hope, and rebellion.",
